1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,680
Hello everyone and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:06,440
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:07,980
And my name is Catherine.

4
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:12,250
And today we're going to be visiting one of my favorite places, I must say.

5
00:00:12,690 --> 00:00:13,030
Really?

6
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:15,160
So you're a big meat eater?

7
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:15,590
I am.

8
00:00:15,730 --> 00:00:20,070
I have to have meat every day, so that's why I usually go to the butcher.

9
00:00:20,670 --> 00:00:20,850
All right.

10
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:26,790
So vegetarians be warned, today's dialogue is all about eating meat and where to go to get meat.

11
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:29,010
So as Marco said, that place is called the butcher's.

12
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:34,010
So we're going to be visiting the butcher and we're going to learn all the different types of meat you can find there.

13
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:35,520
Great.

14
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:44,360
So we don't have anything to preview, but before we get started, just like to mention that you're going to hear a lot of different kinds of words and we'll be back in a minute to talk about what they mean.

15
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:57,680
Hi, what can I get for you?

16
00:00:57,680 --> 00:00:59,520
I'd like half pound of ground beef, please.

17
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:00,980
Good choice.

18
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:06,480
Our ground beef is extra lean, if you know what I mean.

19
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:11,940
Could I also have half a dozen pork chops and two pounds of boneless chicken breast?

20
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:17,540
No, no, no chicken breasts at the moment.

21
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:21,960
But we have some nice chicken thighs.

22
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:23,820
No, that won't do.

23
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:26,140
I'll take this smoked ham you have here.

24
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:29,960
Sorry, is there anything else?

25
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:32,340
Is this salami and bologna you have here?

26
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:36,660
Yes, it's very fine meat.

27
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:38,200
Made it myself.

28
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:40,100
Sounds good.

29
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:41,220
Okay, that's it.

30
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:46,960
Wait, we have T-bone, rib eye and sirloin steaks.

31
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:49,160
They are very fresh.

32
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:51,700
Just came from the slaughterhouse.

33
00:01:52,690 --> 00:01:54,480
No, that's okay.

34
00:01:55,170 --> 00:01:56,750
Really, I think that's all for today.

35
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:06,160
Okay, that'll be $34.50.

36
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:10,920
All right, we're back.

37
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:13,340
So this butcher was kind of creepy in the beginning.

38
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:15,560
Very creepy in the beginning.

39
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:23,990
I think that's usually the conception that you have of butchers because they always have a knife and they're always covered in blood, but it's not human blood.

40
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:26,130
Right, like the movie Sweeney Todd.

41
00:02:26,470 --> 00:02:26,890
Did you see that?

42
00:02:26,890 --> 00:02:27,850
Yeah, like Sweeney Todd.

43
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:32,240
The butcher of Fleet Street.

44
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:38,000
But we actually took a look at a lot of different types of meat that the woman was ordering.

45
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,430
So why don't we take a look at these now in Language Takeaway.

46
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:43,180
Language Takeaway.

47
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:47,400
Okay, so the first is a classic cut.

48
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:50,360
This is something that we had at my house about once a week growing up.

49
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:51,280
It's called a pork chop.

50
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:52,320
Pork chop.

51
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:56,680
So a pork chop comes from the animal, the pork or pig.

52
00:02:57,060 --> 00:02:58,900
Yeah, we actually don't say pork to mean the animal.

53
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:01,660
Pork just means the kind of meat that comes from a pig.

54
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:05,520
So big difference there when we're talking about food, we say pork.

55
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:06,520
Oh, wow.

56
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:08,000
And what about a pork chop?

57
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:09,360
So what exactly is that?

58
00:03:09,710 --> 00:03:21,950
Chop is usually just on the bone and oftentimes it's fried or baked, I guess, but it's just a piece of pork on the bone and a chop, you just imagine a knife chopping something.

59
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:25,880
All right, so a pork chop, very good.

60
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:29,170
And after pork chops, we were ordering some ground beef.

61
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:33,040
All right, so ground beef is another important one.

62
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:43,400
Ground beef is beef that's been chopped up and pushed through a machine that kind of pushes it out in very, very small bits and pieces.

63
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:43,650
There's no bones.

64
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:46,250
So ground comes from the verb to grind.

65
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:50,430
So to grind up meat, to make it very, very small pieces.

66
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:56,930
Right, so if you want to make a spaghetti bolognese, the kind of beef you use is ground beef or even hamburgers.

67
00:03:57,320 --> 00:03:58,840
Oh, yeah, exactly.

68
00:03:58,840 --> 00:04:01,420
So ground beef, you ground up beef.

69
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:02,450
What other things would you grind?

70
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:05,810
You could grind your teeth.

71
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:09,650
Some people do that when they're sleeping, they grind their teeth.

72
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:13,490
You can grind, you have pepper grinders.

73
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:18,760
You know, peppers sometimes come in balls and you have to grind them to make them smaller and broken down.

74
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:19,110
Very good.

75
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:23,190
So that's ground beef and from the verb to grind.

76
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:27,750
Now we are buying some boneless chicken breasts.

77
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:33,440
All right, so boneless, as you have guessed, means without bones.

78
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:46,720
And chicken breasts are usually just a really nice, juicy, kind of tender cut of meat from the chicken, from the underside of the chicken where obviously, yeah, they have it.

79
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:48,830
It's not a wing, it's not a leg, it's just a chicken breast.

80
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:51,120
All right, so boneless chicken breast.

81
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:53,790
Now usually it is boneless, right, when you buy it at the supermarket.

82
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:55,560
Yeah, yeah.

83
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:59,360
Next up, we've got some classic deli counter favorites.

84
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:00,610
These are our cold cuts.

85
00:05:01,230 --> 00:05:02,670
All right, so cold cuts.

86
00:05:03,130 --> 00:05:04,970
Now this means that they are cold?

87
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:07,600
Oh, well, yeah, they're cold.

88
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:15,380
But I think cold cuts, the most important thing to remember here is that they're mostly like kinds of salami or cured meats.

89
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:18,920
So these are salt cured or they're dried meats.

90
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:22,460
And you usually have them on sandwiches, so like a salami sandwich.

91
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:25,720
So like cured meat, you mean that meat hasn't been cooked?

92
00:05:26,330 --> 00:05:31,170
Oh, no, it's not been cooked, but it's treated in a way that allows it to last for a very long time.

93
00:05:31,610 --> 00:05:35,210
So they look like long, long tubes of meat.

94
00:05:35,750 --> 00:05:36,970
They look very dry.

95
00:05:37,310 --> 00:05:39,110
They look very dry, like beef jerky, for example.

96
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:40,840
All right, so cured meat.

97
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:45,160
Now, for example, we have salami and bologna.

98
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:46,160
Bologna.

99
00:05:46,550 --> 00:05:52,440
Now, it's interesting because bologna is actually spelled B-O-L-O-G-N-A.

100
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:55,080
So you would say bologna or something like that.

101
00:05:55,080 --> 00:05:57,050
Well, I think it comes from the Italian bologna.

102
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:03,280
But it's definitely a change that happened in the English language when it came over from Italy.

103
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:07,840
But bologna is a kind of meat that's got a very funny looking name.

104
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:10,160
Yeah, see, you're more sophisticated than I am.

105
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:11,160
That's why you know this difference.

106
00:06:11,160 --> 00:06:12,160
I just speak Italian.

107
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:17,680
All right, so you have salami, bologna, and...

108
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:18,600
Pastrami.

109
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:19,080
Pastrami.

110
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:20,680
What about ham?

111
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:21,680
Is that considered a cold cut?

112
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:26,560
You can have ham in the cold cut section because ham sometimes comes in tubes that you slice.

113
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:29,880
But ham is also just a normal kind of pork.

114
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:31,520
But ham is cured, you know.

115
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:32,280
Like a smoked ham.

116
00:06:32,620 --> 00:06:32,920
Exactly.

117
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:34,960
Or smoked turkey, one of my favorites.

118
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:36,140
Very good.

119
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:41,480
And, well, now moving on from the cold cuts, we're going to concentrate on steaks.

120
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:42,980
Ooh, steaks.

121
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:48,240
So there are a thousand kinds of steaks in this world and every city seems to have its own kind.

122
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:51,520
But here we're only looking at three, the first of which is the T-bone.

123
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:55,240
All right, the T-bone steak, I think, is one of the most popular ones.

124
00:06:55,440 --> 00:06:55,640
Definitely.

125
00:06:56,480 --> 00:06:58,220
It's a steak that's pretty big, right?

126
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:00,140
Yeah, well, yeah, it can be.

127
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:04,480
And it has a bone inside that looks like a T.

128
00:07:04,520 --> 00:07:10,880
All right, so you're saying, Marco, that the T-bone is named after the shape and the look of this steak.

129
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:13,160
Yeah, T-bone steak.

130
00:07:13,160 --> 00:07:13,380
All right.

131
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:16,640
And as well as our next one is also a rib eye.

132
00:07:16,920 --> 00:07:21,120
Okay, so the rib eye, I think, actually has a piece of the rib in it.

133
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:28,380
So the rib is the bone and there's a circle, which is kind of a, it's, it's a, the piece of meat is sliced off the rib and the circle is actually part of that rib.

134
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:29,880
So there's a little bit of bone in it.

135
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:33,920
But it's not as bony as the T-bone.

136
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:34,040
Right.

137
00:07:34,680 --> 00:07:38,420
So it does have one small bone that kind of looks like an eye because you can see through it.

138
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:45,660
And our next steak has no bone and is the most tender and the best steak you can have, sirloin steak.

139
00:07:45,940 --> 00:07:49,020
So I think the, the sirloin is definitely the most expensive if you're in a restaurant.

140
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:52,000
Definitely the most tender, I think, of all those.

141
00:07:52,320 --> 00:07:53,320
It's delicious.

142
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:58,740
And usually you can have like a big sirloin steak, like a half a kilo, like a 400 gram sirloin steak.

143
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:00,600
I had a friend who ate a full kilo.

144
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:00,980
Really?

145
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:01,880
Sirloin steak.

146
00:08:02,280 --> 00:08:02,400
Wow.

147
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:04,340
Is there a competition or?

148
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:09,100
No, there's a kind of steak called la bistecca alla fiorentina in Italy.

149
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:14,320
It's the Florentine beef steak and it is, it can be about a kilo of meat.

150
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:15,000
Wow.

151
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:16,800
Yeah.

152
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:18,120
I bet you can't even move after that.

153
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:22,240
No, you're, no, you fall asleep with your head in your soup on the table.

154
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:22,560
All right.

155
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:26,580
So that's all the meat related words that we have for you today.

156
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:32,040
So why don't we move on now to Fluency Builder where we're going to take a look at some of the phrases in this dialogue.

157
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:34,330
Fluency Builder.

158
00:08:35,700 --> 00:08:38,090
We're going to look at the phrases if you know what I mean.

159
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:39,310
If you know what I mean.

160
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,430
Now I love this phrase, if you know what I mean.

161
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:44,030
I do know what you mean.

162
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:45,200
Well, it's weird.

163
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:46,760
I like it too.

164
00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:48,550
So you can use it for sarcasm.

165
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:49,960
Yeah.

166
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:50,960
Right.

167
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:52,350
Or you can use it to kind of insinuate something.

168
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:53,720
Right.

169
00:08:53,720 --> 00:08:54,720
Exactly.

170
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:55,720
Yeah.

171
00:08:55,720 --> 00:08:56,720
So here's what you're saying.

172
00:08:56,720 --> 00:08:58,900
You're saying there's a double meaning in what I want to say.

173
00:08:58,900 --> 00:09:06,600
So there's two things I could say and I'm not going to say it outright, but you know, this thing that we can't really say.

174
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:11,800
So for example, we can say she likes to go out if you know what I mean.

175
00:09:12,200 --> 00:09:12,400
All right.

176
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:20,240
So you're insinuating that not only does she like to go out, but maybe, maybe she goes out a lot, a lot, or maybe she gets drunk or something like that.

177
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:21,600
Yeah.

178
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:24,140
So it's kind of like a double meaning, but you're trying to be subtle.

179
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:24,880
Yeah.

180
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:28,740
But it's not so subtle because you're actually saying like, this is not what I mean.

181
00:09:30,040 --> 00:09:32,540
Or she's eating for two if you know what I mean.

182
00:09:32,940 --> 00:09:33,440
There we go.

183
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:37,480
So you're insinuating that, well, you're not really insinuating, you're telling me that.

184
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:37,960
She's pregnant.

185
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:45,720
Eating for two is a common idiom in English that means that a person, a woman has a, well, is going to have a baby.

186
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:48,060
So here I'm saying, hey, do you know this idiom?

187
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:50,560
She's eating for two if you know what I mean.

188
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:51,880
Right.

189
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:52,880
Wink, wink, wink.

190
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:53,880
Wink, wink.

191
00:09:53,880 --> 00:10:03,720
So now why don't we take a look at some other examples of how we could use if you know what I mean in different circumstances when you're trying to insinuate something.

192
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:05,760
Example one.

193
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:09,920
She's eating for two now, if you know what I mean.

194
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:10,920
Example two.

195
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:16,520
Tim hit a home run today, if you know what I mean.

196
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:19,000
Example three.

197
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,320
Jim and Laura are going their separate ways, if you know what I mean.

198
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:24,360
All right.

199
00:10:24,870 --> 00:10:25,230
We're back.

200
00:10:25,450 --> 00:10:28,670
So now that that's clear, why don't we move on to our next phrase?

201
00:10:29,230 --> 00:10:30,670
So this one is a counting phrase.

202
00:10:30,870 --> 00:10:33,890
And for those of you who have heard the word dozen before, it might not be so new.

203
00:10:34,370 --> 00:10:35,710
The phrase is half a dozen.

204
00:10:35,890 --> 00:10:36,550
Half a dozen.

205
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:39,300
So that means half of 12.

206
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:40,680
12.

207
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:41,240
So a dozen is 12.

208
00:10:42,860 --> 00:10:49,720
And it's, I'm not really sure when this started being used in English, but a dozen is 12 and half a dozen is then six.

209
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,860
Now there's another phrase or another saying that's very common, a dime a dozen.

210
00:10:55,640 --> 00:10:56,700
He's a dime a dozen.

211
00:10:57,360 --> 00:11:01,380
So that means you can get 12 of the same type of guys for 10 cents.

212
00:11:02,120 --> 00:11:03,120
Right.

213
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:04,130
So they're easy to find.

214
00:11:04,360 --> 00:11:05,010
They're easy to find.

215
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:06,360
Yeah.

216
00:11:06,360 --> 00:11:07,360
The dime a dozen.

217
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:08,360
All right.

218
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:09,360
Very good.

219
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:10,360
And moving on to our last one.

220
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:14,450
When they were talking, they said something about the meat being lean, extra lean.

221
00:11:15,090 --> 00:11:15,250
Okay.

222
00:11:15,530 --> 00:11:18,450
Lean, this does not mean lean on a door.

223
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:21,160
This is not like resting on something.

224
00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:24,960
This is actually a kind of way to describe food.

225
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:29,600
And so food is lean if it's not got much fat, it's not fatty.

226
00:11:29,730 --> 00:11:29,950
Okay.

227
00:11:29,990 --> 00:11:32,780
So if it's lean, it doesn't have much fat.

228
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:33,480
Exactly.

229
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:35,680
So I prefer to eat lean meats.

230
00:11:35,680 --> 00:11:38,640
So I often eat birds and poultry.

231
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:40,760
So chicken, I like it pretty lean.

232
00:11:40,760 --> 00:11:42,760
And if it's got fat on it, I'll cut the fat off.

233
00:11:43,120 --> 00:11:44,120
Oh, okay.

234
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:45,120
Like the skin.

235
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:45,820
The skin is fat, right?

236
00:11:46,120 --> 00:11:47,120
Yeah.

237
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:48,120
All right.

238
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:49,120
So you can have lean beef.

239
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:50,680
It doesn't have much fat around it.

240
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:50,960
Yeah.

241
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:57,640
And in America actually on the packages at the supermarket, it often says the percentage of meat that you have.

242
00:11:57,640 --> 00:12:00,620
So it's 95% lean or 98% lean.

243
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:02,660
So that means you have 3% fat in there.

244
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:03,080
Exactly.

245
00:12:04,440 --> 00:12:05,440
Or 2%.

246
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:06,840
My math skills are amazing.

247
00:12:06,840 --> 00:12:07,840
All right.

248
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:13,980
So now that we've finished with all of these words on Fluency Builder, let's take a look at our dialogue for the last time.

249
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:42,560
Hi, what can I get for you?

250
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:52,640
No, no, no chicken breasts at the moment, but we have some nice chicken thighs.

251
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:53,490
No that won't do.

252
00:12:54,160 --> 00:12:55,830
I'll take this smoked ham you have here.

253
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:57,560
Okay.

254
00:12:57,560 --> 00:12:58,550
Is there anything else?

255
00:12:59,640 --> 00:13:02,030
Is this salami and bologna you have here?

256
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:04,680
Yes.

257
00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:07,400
It's very fine meat.

258
00:13:07,400 --> 00:13:07,950
Made it myself.

259
00:13:09,640 --> 00:13:09,790
Sounds good.

260
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:10,910
Okay, that's it.

261
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:12,720
Great.

262
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:17,240
We have T-bone, rib eye, and sirloin steaks.

263
00:13:17,580 --> 00:13:18,680
They are very fresh.

264
00:13:19,880 --> 00:13:22,240
Just came from the slaughterhouse.

265
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:25,100
No, that's okay, really.

266
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:26,400
I think that's all for today.

267
00:13:30,680 --> 00:13:35,600
Okay, that'll be $34.50.

268
00:13:35,600 --> 00:13:41,880
Alright, we're back.

269
00:13:41,880 --> 00:13:44,970
So Catherine, are you a big beef or meat eater?

270
00:13:45,350 --> 00:13:46,110
Definitely am.

271
00:13:46,330 --> 00:13:55,110
It helps that my partner in crime is from Kansas City and they have the best, among the best steaks in our entire country in Kansas City.

272
00:13:55,520 --> 00:13:56,520
Wow.

273
00:13:56,520 --> 00:14:01,800
I actually miss, there was back in California, we had the $6 burger from Carl's Jr.

274
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:11,240
It was just basically beef, bacon, cheese, it was just like this huge burger full of meat and obviously a lot of calories.

275
00:14:11,240 --> 00:14:11,930
But it was delicious.

276
00:14:13,480 --> 00:14:18,120
In Ecuador we are very used to having some meat product every day with our food.

277
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:23,640
Yeah, well it's the same in the Midwest actually from where we're from.

278
00:14:23,640 --> 00:14:30,040
It's the same in the Midwest because people in the Midwest generally think that if you're not eating meat then what are you eating?

279
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:31,800
Bread?

280
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:32,800
Right.

281
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:33,800
Corn?

282
00:14:33,800 --> 00:14:34,800
Yeah.

283
00:14:34,800 --> 00:14:38,360
So meat is considered a healthy part of the day and you definitely eat it with your meals.

284
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:44,560
Yeah, and I'm sure our vegetarian listeners might not appreciate this but it's just kind of different diets.

285
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,640
Some people eat, for example, rice every day.

286
00:14:47,640 --> 00:14:50,960
So where in other countries you wouldn't really have rice every day.

287
00:14:50,960 --> 00:14:54,240
Or for example, when I was in Chile, I had a lot of potatoes.

288
00:14:54,580 --> 00:14:54,920
Really?

289
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:55,780
A lot of potatoes.

290
00:14:56,320 --> 00:14:57,660
Have you ever had an Argentine steak?

291
00:14:57,800 --> 00:14:59,800
I hear they have the best beef in the world.

292
00:14:59,940 --> 00:15:03,840
They are not only delicious but they are very inexpensive and it's amazing.

293
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:15,520
You can have a complete steak buffet for like, I don't know, I can't even remember but I sat there for three hours and couldn't move and kept eating.

294
00:15:15,520 --> 00:15:17,020
Is it true you can eat it with a spoon?

295
00:15:17,680 --> 00:15:18,140
It's so soft?

296
00:15:18,680 --> 00:15:20,480
Some of the meat is very, very tender.

297
00:15:20,480 --> 00:15:24,220
Not if it's grilled, you can't really do that, but in other preparations it's almost like that.

298
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:25,380
With a nice butter sauce.

299
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:27,000
Mmm, herbs.

300
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:28,000
Making me hungry.

301
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,800
I know, you've made me hungry.

302
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:35,720
I think we're going to take off and have some burgers.

303
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:41,790
If you have any questions, any comments, please come to our website at EnglishPod.com and we're there to answer any doubts.

304
00:15:42,520 --> 00:15:46,490
And we'd love to know what your favorite foods are, meat or otherwise, so post something on our website.

305
00:15:47,080 --> 00:15:47,590
Hope to see you there soon.

306
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:49,080
Bye.
